\chapter{Who owns the result? A cleanup lab}
\label{app:ownership}
\index{Ownership!tracing a returned owner}
\index{Cleanup!observing live boxes}
\index{Result!consuming propagation}

A constructor allocates two boxes, discovers a problem, and returns
an error code. Just before it returns, a diagnostic prints ``2 boxes
live.'' Is that a leak? A count alone cannot tell us. We need to know
who owns the boxes \emph{when the count is taken}. This lab puts
observers on either side of the return.

Use the direct C11 compiler built in \cref{ch:3}. The input is a
regression fixture from the repository. It uses the private runtime
counter \OnxCodeTerm{on1x\_unique\_live\_allocations()} to count
outstanding unique boxes. This is a test instrument: a zero tells us
about boxes, not about all the memory used by the process.

\section{Choose the instant to measure}
The constructor below calls \OnxCodeTerm{node(n)} to box a
\OnxCodeTerm{Node} carrying the integer \OnxCodeTerm{n}. Its
\OnxCodeTerm{fail\_after} argument selects an ordinary return:
1 means stop after the first acquisition, 2 means stop after the
second, and 0 means finish. Both calls to \OnxCodeTerm{node} can
succeed even on a path that returns an error.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=17,lastline=38,firstnumber=17]{../tests/fixtures/owning_result_lifecycle.on1x}

Notice where the parentheses are. Registering
\OnxCodeTerm{defer report(100 + fail\_after);} saves the stage
number. The call to the allocation counter is \emph{inside}
\OnxCodeTerm{report}; it runs when the constructor exits. If the
constructor instead deferred a print of the counter itself, it would
save the count at registration, before allocating the second node.

Before reading on, predict the count at stages 100, 101, and 102.
Count a box transferred into a pending return until the caller
actually consumes it.

\section{Follow the payload into the caller}
\OnxInputCode[firstline=40,lastline=50,firstnumber=40]{../tests/fixtures/owning_result_lifecycle.on1x}

The two function signatures tell us what \OnxCodeTerm{?} can do:
\OnxCodeTerm{construct} supplies either an owner or a \OnxCodeTerm{u8}
error. \OnxCodeTerm{consume} promises an integer on success but the
same error type on failure. Draw two paths from \OnxCodeTerm{pending?}.
One leads to \OnxCodeTerm{take(owner)}, which frees the box and gives
the function its \OnxCodeTerm{Node} payload. The other leads straight
to a return carrying the error byte. Both pass through the deferred
observer belonging to \OnxCodeTerm{consume}.

Run the complete fixture from the MuLIB repository root:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
on1xc=build/on1x/on1xc
fixtures=on1x/tests/fixtures
src=$fixtures/owning_result_lifecycle.on1x
"$on1xc" --check "$src"
python3 on1x/tools/on1x-build.py "$src" --on1xc "$on1xc" \
  -o build/owning-result
build/owning-result
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Read the output in groups of five lines. Each \OnxCodeTerm{report}
prints its stage and then the live count; \OnxCodeTerm{main} prints
the returned number or error. Here each row groups one call:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{r|rr|rr|r}
 & \multicolumn{2}{c|}{Constructor} & \multicolumn{2}{c|}{Consumer}
 & Caller \\
Input & Stage & Live & Stage & Live & Prints \\
\hline
0 & 100 & 1 & 200 & 0 & 20 \\
1 & 101 & 1 & 201 & 0 & 7 \\
2 & 102 & 2 & 202 & 0 & 8
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The first two constructor counts are identical for different reasons.
At 100, \OnxCodeTerm{first} is already gone and the saved
\OnxCodeTerm{Ok} owns the second box. At 101, \OnxCodeTerm{first}
is still a local and the saved \OnxCodeTerm{Err} owns no box at all.
At 102, both boxes are locals. They are dropped after the constructor's
defer runs, before control returns to \OnxCodeTerm{consume}. There is
no leak implied by the 2. Conversely, a 0 at stage 200 would be poor
evidence on its own: the 20 printed by the caller also checks that the
intended payload made it out.

\section{An error can own a value, too}
There is one line of output left: 9. The fixture's
\OnxCodeTerm{owned\_failure} creates a \OnxCodeTerm{Failure} box
carrying that number and places it in \OnxCodeTerm{Err}.
\OnxCodeTerm{forward\_failure} applies \OnxCodeTerm{?}. Its declared
success type is \OnxCodeTerm{bool}, but the conversion from an
integer to a Boolean is never reached. The active payload is the
error, so the caller receives the box itself.

If propagating meant destroying every local \emph{including} what it
contained at entry, the final match could not read 9. Instead the
fixture asserts that one box is live after the return; the error arm
takes the box, prints its number, and the last assertion checks for
zero. This pair of observations tests both halves of the handoff:
the callee did not drop the returned owner, and the caller did not
leave it live.

\section{Change one lifetime}
Make a working copy of the fixture:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
cp "$src" build/early-drop.on1x
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
Move the single \OnxCodeTerm{drop(first);} in \OnxCodeTerm{construct}
to immediately after its \OnxCodeTerm{defer report(...);} line.
Which column of the table can change? Rebuild with this source:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
python3 on1x/tools/on1x-build.py build/early-drop.on1x \
  --on1xc "$on1xc" -o build/early-drop
build/early-drop
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
The constructor counts are now 1, 0, and 1. On success the first box
was dropped explicitly in both versions. On each error path its
lifetime has become shorter. The consumer counts and returned values
stay the same. Moving the drop changes what the observer can see,
even though a test of just the three results would see no difference.

\section{Try to extract the same owner twice}
\OnxInputCode[firstline=18,lastline=26,firstnumber=18]{../tests/fixtures/owning_result_double_propagate.on1x}
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
"$on1xc" --check "$fixtures/owning_result_double_propagate.on1x"
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
Expect a nonzero exit and \OnxCodeTerm{use after move of local: pending}
at the \emph{second} question mark. The first one consumes the named
result. Dropping \OnxCodeTerm{first} destroys the extracted box; it
cannot refill \OnxCodeTerm{pending}. Even an empty \OnxCodeTerm{main}
does not hide the error in this function. If two calculations need the
number, take the box once, match the node once, and use the copyable
integer in both calculations.

For another way to inspect the successful handoff, emit C for the
original fixture:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
"$on1xc" --emit-c "$src" -o build/owning-result.c
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
Find the definition of \OnxCodeTerm{on1x\_fn\_construct} and start
at its last return. Work backwards: the defer ran with a return value
already saved; the second local's storage was cleared when its owner
moved. A cleanup call on that cleared slot does not destroy the box
in the return value. Follow the operations rather than memorizing
the generated temporary names.

All three inputs here model a decision made by the program. Failure
of \OnxCodeTerm{box} itself is different: the runtime's owning
allocation failure path is terminal, not another \OnxCodeTerm{Err}
arm. The companion test injects allocator failures and checks exit
status 72 separately from the recoverable results above.

